ut a conversation, and how it feels. This isn't going to work if treat nuance as nonsense and ignore it.<br /> <br /> To further elaborate a bit here: you seem to be confusing &quot;nuance&quot; and &quot;subtext.&quot; Which is fair, they're similar concepts in practice. Nuance is literal, but subtle and contextual. Nuance is in essence about make small distinctions (though small does not mean unimportant). When discussing technical matters, nuance is usually in contrast to abstraction or generalization. In a technical conversation topics A and B might be by far the most common outcomes. So one speaker might decide to solely focus on A and B. Another speaker might decide the topics C and D are less common outcomes, but despite being less seen, are significant enough to be worth addressing as well. The second speaker (if one agrees C and D merit discussion) would be more nuanced for including the distinctions C and D offer.<br /> <br /> In less formal discussions, nuance usually means distinctions in word choice. I could describe someone who makes very long and wordy posts as either loquacious or droning. Both do literally mean that they are communicating in a lengthy fashion. But the nuance is in the most specific meanings of those words. Loquacious is more neutral, though implies someone has the vocabulary to keep up with number of word they're using. Droning is an overtly negative description of a long piece, as it means that the words have started to blur together as the audience loses interest (a drone in this case is the sound of lots of individuals coming together to make one larger sound you can't hear the individual parts of; such as a swarm of bees buzzing as one, or the sounds of all the activity of an active urban city). Both words are literal in usage: if you look them up in an English dictionary, you can see they might have a broad overlapping surface meaning, but they are not neutrally interchangeable. Picking which you use is being nuanced.<br /> <br /> Subtext is when the meaning of a phrase is not available from the literal meaning of the words. The meaning is &quot;under&quot; the text, as in there is another layer you need to know to truly understand what the author intends to communicate. In vocal communicate subtext is usually done via non-verbal communication: body posture, specific emphasis on words or syllables, and so on. &quot;I think it's time to leave,&quot; based on context could have subtext to really mean:<br /> <br /> &quot;I think it's time for you to leave as you've overstayed your welcome.&quot;<br /> &quot;I am reminding you this is the time you wanted to leave by.&quot;<br /> &quot;I think we should leave now because social circumstances have made staying uncomfortable.&quot;<br /> <br /> Those, and more, are where subtext comes in. The literal face value of the statement creates noise instead of clarity because of the different ways the phrase can come off based on non-literal context cues. Idioms, metaphors, and cultural touchstones tend make subtext even harder to handle in translation. This is where the two tend to overlap. If I describe one person's write as &quot;evocative&quot; and another's as &quot;purple&quot; the purple statement causes confusion because purple is a color. To find the nuanced distinction you would have to know the reference to the concept of &quot;purple prose&quot; to find the definition of &quot;purple&quot; that wouldn't be found in most dictionaries under just purple itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-26T08:20:38-07:00","id"