er of word they're using. Droning is an overtly negative description of a long piece, as it means that the words have started to blur together as the audience loses interest (a drone in this case is the sound of lots of individuals coming together to make one larger sound you can't hear the individual parts of; such as a swarm of bees buzzing as one, or the sounds of all the activity of an active urban city). Both words are literal in usage: if you look them up in an English dictionary, you can see they might have a broad overlapping surface meaning, but they are not neutrally interchangeable. Picking which you use is being nuanced.<br /> <br /> Subtext is when the meaning of a phrase is not available from the literal meaning of the words. The meaning is &quot;under&quot; the text, as in there is another layer you need to know to truly understand what the author intends to communicate. In vocal communicate subtext is usually done via non-verbal communication: body posture, specific emphasis on words or syllables, and so on. &quot;I think it's time to leave,&quot; based on context could have subtext to really mean:<br /> <br /> &quot;I think it's time for you to leave as you've overstayed your welcome.&quot;<br /> &quot;I am reminding you this is the time you wanted to leave by.&quot;<br /> &quot;I think we should leave now because social circumstances have made staying uncomfortable.&quot;<br /> <br /> Those, and more, are where subtext comes in. The literal face value of the statement creates noise instead of clarity because of the different ways the phrase can come off based on non-literal context cues. Idioms, metaphors, and cultural touchstones tend make subtext even harder to handle in translation. This is where the two tend to overlap. If I describe one person's write as &quot;evocative&quot; and another's as &quot;purple&quot; the purple statement causes confusion because purple is a color. To find the nuanced distinction you would have to know the reference to the concept of &quot;purple prose&quot; to find the definition of &quot;purple&quot; that wouldn't be found in most dictionaries under just purple itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, I guess I get the general idea, and it's still a pretty big &quot;I guess&quot;, although that &quot;I think is time to live&quot; is rarely used around here as it's seen as gross rudeness. Anyway, going back to the original text, what I want to say is that I can guarantee you that there is no subtext in this dialogue, it is very literal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-26T14:02:10-07:00","id":1518844,"title":"","content":"It's impossible for human communication to lack subtext. Subtext is an inherent aspect of the fact that we cannot perfectly beam our ideas into the heads of other people with 100% fidelity to our intended meaning.<br /> <br /> There might not be purposeful subtext - like my &quot;it's time to leave&quot; examples - but accidental subtext is still possible. Even things as simple as choosing to have the conversation in front of a crowd of people, vs. pulling someone aside to have the conversation alone, is a form of subtext, because it can change the context of the communication.<br /> <br /> Going back to the idea of the Bone Gnawer not asking for a hot-dog: there's subtext (since it's not literally stated in the dialogue) that the Bone Gnawer is fine with getting less than everyone else just because the Silver Fang is right that the Bone Gnawer wouldn't value a more expensive item due to it lack in utility other than as a means to get a more practical item and have money for other things. Neither of them said that, it doesn't contradict what either said, but it's clearly there because it's a matter of fact in the situation if we accept that both characters are being as honest and literal as possible.<br /> <br /> That unaddressed subtext feels very wrong. And there would be more subtext depending on things like if other characters are aware of the value of the gift the Silver Fang got the Bone Gnawer compared to the value of other gifts handed out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-31T08:22:04-07:00","id":1519252,"title":"","content":"And why SF bought all those things? If it's because she's a kind leader and wanted to share these things with all others as her loyal subjects I would feel offended if I was that BG. &quot;Why she gave me trash&quot; and so on. Just like my father once did: he wanted to buy me a band t-shirt and chose a band that I don't like. I understood his intentions but still I felt slightly offended. After all he showed me my favourite band so he should know what t-shirt he should buy to make this gift tailored for me. But if the food is for everyone and gifts are personal I would take it as a friendly ribbing. She wanted to give them something that they would keep and cherish. So their personal relationship and context is important.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Warrior of the Rainbow<br /> Saint among the sinners<br /> Pure among the dirt<br /> Loser among winners","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13141-nail-eater","name":"Nail Eater","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-03T04:35:20-07:00","id":1519602,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1518727-wrong-dialogue?p=1518785#post1518785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It Isn't Mock, She spoke her mind and he agreed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which can happen, but it's just sheer luck. Pure chance.<br /> In a real world context especially. Not so much in a movie or other tight narrative.<br /> It feels a dialogue from a movie where you don't have time to build up much context, and you want a few cool one-liners.<br /> I'm believing that's why we're all prone to imagine dialogues like that. We watched too much TV ^^<br /> <br /> Note: I didn't mean to criticize the dialogue, I thought it was funny enough and it made me imagine the two characters doing that.<br /> And without context I'd say it's good enough already.<br /> Then the other comments about context made me imagine what the relationship and past of the two of them could be.<br /> Which again is good.<br /> But if you're asking if there is &quot;wrongness&quot; in a more realism mindset instead of pure narrative, the others already gave you good opinions about it saying that context is key","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-03T19:30:22-07:00","id":1519697,"title":"","content":"Only the two individuals having the conversation can really speak to subtext, either intended or percieved, with any authority. In a written context like this, the writer can tell us on their behalf what each speaker intended and how much of that intention each listener understood (or misunderstood!).<br /> <br /> But here, we explicitly *don't* have any of that context, we just have the raw text. So, we're all left to assume, and the general sentiment seems to be that absent other context, the SF seems patronizing (somewhere between &quot;mildly&quot; and &quot;extremely&quot; offensive) but the BG seems unconcerned by it.<br /> <br /> Depending on the context of their relationship, primarily if we know the characters *do* already respect one another and share a degree of comfort that allows for unadorned pragmatism, this could well be good-natured ribbing, but if we're saying they're 100% earnest... then they both come off as socially oblivious. The SF doesn't realize how patronizing she's being, and the BG doesn't realize he's being talked down to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}